ey wound up in Germany  they thought there were some  opportunities in Germany and they wound  up in Augsburg Germany that's where you  were born that's where I was born and my  sister my older sister was born in  Augsburg too and my dad often told the  story to me and others that one day he  was a nearby Munich which is not far  from Oxford and he sees a long long line  he said in those days after the war you  see a long line you go wait in line  there must be something good at the end  of the line if all these people are  waiting in line so he went and waited at  the end of the line  and after about a half an hour he said  to the woman in front of him Frey line  by the way why are we waiting in line  and she looked at him and she said I  thought you knew it he said no he said  we're waiting in line for visas my dad  said visas for what she said America  what my dad didn't know  was that the U.S House of  Representatives in the U.S Senate had  just passed what was called the  displaced persons Act of 1948 which  granted 400 000 visas to displaced  persons including Holocaust Survivors  refugees you know my parents were always  so grateful to this country for giving  them an opportunity to start a new life  and raise a family become successful and  they became very patriotic Americans in  the process  well thank you so very much and also for  this documentary absolutely worth  watching and all the best thank you  thank you very much thank you  and thanks to all you're doing
 

